# Exceptions for .asset files such as lightning pre-baking
LightingData.asset     binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# *.asset is deliberately NOT given the unity-yaml macro above.
#
# That macro carries eol=lf, which normalises line endings even on files git
# detects as binary. Most .asset files are YAML under Force Text serialization,
# but some types - TerrainData above all - stay BINARY whatever the
# serialization mode. Applying eol=lf to those strips every CR byte out of the
# file and destroys it silently: the file still opens, still has a plausible
# size, and Unity simply refuses to import it.
#
# This project lost a sculpted 1025x1025 terrain heightmap that way on
# 2026-08-15. Assets/New Terrain 2.asset went into git 1,070 bytes short of the
# size its own header declared, and restoring it from the commit overwrote the
# only good copy. Assets/New Terrain.asset had already been damaged the same
# way, 59 bytes short, since the clone on 2026-07-28.
#
# Leaving the attribute off lets git auto-detect: YAML .asset files are handled
# as text, binary ones are left byte-for-byte alone. Do not add eol or text
# here without checking round-trip integrity on a binary .asset first.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.asset                 merge=unityyamlmerge linguist-language=yaml

# Known-binary .asset files, declared rather than left to auto-detection.
#
# Auto-detect gets these right today - they open with NUL bytes, so git classifies
# them as binary and leaves them alone. But that is a heuristic reading the first
# 8000 bytes, and core.autocrlf is true on this project's machines. For the one
# file type that has already been destroyed once, say it outright.
#
# Add new TerrainData assets here as they are created.
/Assets/Terrain/LavaWorld_Terrain.asset  -text
/Assets/Terrain/FarmWorld_Terrain.asset  -text

# The loose terrain assets at the Assets root - the ones the incident above was about.
#
# These are the worst case for auto-detection, and worse than the note above implies:
# they are binary, but carry no NUL byte in the first 8000 bytes, so git's heuristic
# classifies them as TEXT. With core.autocrlf=true that means CR bytes get stripped on
# checkin. "New Terrain 2.asset" is binary from its first byte and git calls it text -
# checked 2026-08-21, which is why these are declared rather than left to the heuristic.
"/Assets/New Terrain.asset"              -text
"/Assets/New Terrain 1.asset"            -text
"/Assets/New Terrain 2.asset"            -text
"/Assets/New Terrain 2 Restored.asset"   -text

# Windows batch files must keep CRLF - cmd.exe mis-parses LF-only .cmd
# files, splitting labels and for-loops in ways that fail without an error.
*.cmd                   text eol=crlf
*.bat                   text eol=crlf

# Generated mesh libraries: procedural geometry that SceneMeshExtractor lifts out of scene
# files so a scene edit does not rewrite hundreds of thousands of vertices. They are YAML
# .asset files, but they are pure generated bulk - rewritten wholesale whenever a generator
# runs, and never meaningfully hand-merged. LFS keeps that out of the git packfile and
# re-uses unchanged libraries between pushes instead of re-packing them.
#
# This must stay BELOW the *.asset line above: last match wins, and the lfs macro has to
# override unityyamlmerge for these. If two branches disagree on one, regenerate it.
/Assets/GeneratedMeshes/**/*.asset  lfs
